sudo nmap -sS 192.168.1.0/24 -oX network.xml
sudo nmap -sS 192.168.1.0/24 -oN network.txt
xsltproc network.xml -o network.html
xsltproc /usr/share/nmap/nmap.xsl network.xml -o network.html

Short flag/key explanations:-
sudo — run as root (required for -sS on many systems).
nmap — Network Mapper.
-sS — SYN (stealth / half-open) scan.
192.168.1.0/24 — target subnet (hosts .1–.254).
-oX network.xml — write machine-readable XML to network.xml.
-oN network.txt — (optional) write normal human-readable output to network.txt.
xsltproc network.xml -o network.html — transform the XML into network.html using an XSL stylesheet.

Notes / Safety
Only scan networks you own or have explicit permission to scan.
xsltproc uses the stylesheet referenced inside the Nmap XML (usually nmap.xsl). If missing, supply the path to nmap.xsl.
File names used above: network.xml, network.txt, network.html.
